5 5 7 7 5 5 7 5 5 5 7 5 7 5 5 5\nWrong attempts used: 1/8. Demo requests used: 0/8.\nReply with exactly one action JSON on the last line.","reply":"{\"action\":\"request_demos\"}","steps":[{"type":"tool","name":"exec","input":"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport importlib.util\np='/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-openclawlearner-1792544556/attempts/3223be82bb504fb6b3585b3b468eaac7/system/state/openclaw/agents/main/agent/workshop-skills/continual-arc/SKILL.md'\nprint(open(p).read())\nPY","t":1792598753.614,"output":"---\nname: continual-arc\ndescription: Solve Continual-ARC task-id grids by reusing programs, inferring transformations from demos, and validating before submission.\n---\n\n# Continual ARC\n\nUse this procedure for task-id grid puzzles with an available program library.\n\n1. On every instance reply, put exactly one supported action JSON object on the final line. Check `programs/<task id>.py`; when present, run its `transform(grid)` on the test input and submit that exact result before requesting demos. Never edit, replace, or save a task program before the verdict unless the task explicitly requires an interim saved candidate; keep exploratory implementations outside the authoritative task file. After an incorrect verdict, do not repeat the same submission. If the existing program fails, preserve it as the last-known verified version until a replacement passes all received demonstrations; use separate scratch code for hypotheses.\n2. Infer from the whole grid before coding: distinguish object outlines from dividers, find the background using both frequency and spatial role, and list candidate objects, bounds, interiors, and any separate marker shapes. Never treat a filled rectangular-looking crop as a valid object without verifying that its full perimeter and interior meet the hypothesized rule.\n3. For each demo, compare input and output cell-by-cell and record a compact evidence table: edge strokes or bars, other markers, changed regions, preserved boundaries, and each changed marker's fate. Test rule candidates against every demo, distinguishing propagation from border strokes, rays, fills, and marker recoloring; do not infer a task-wide rule from one suggestive example. Explicitly check orientation and direction: horizontal versus vertical, left versus right, toward versus away from an edge, and whether a ray stops at a marker, boundary, or other color. When output dimensions or repeated rows/columns suggest tiling, first test simple exact constructions such as horizontal reflection, vertical reflection, and repeated/reversed source sequence; derive the complete row/column order explicitly from the demo, including where each block begins and ends. Verify candidate output dimensions against the demonstrated expansion ratio before submitting. Request another demo only if its possible outcomes separate hypotheses that would change the test output, and weigh that value against its one-point cost and remaining attempt budget. If evidence is still ambiguous, keep the uncertainty explicit rather than presenting an unsupported rule as validated.\n4. Implement `transform(grid)` in scratch code and run it on every received demo and the exact test input. Require exact reproduction of all demos before replacing the task file or claiming the candidate is validated; check changed and unchanged cells, especially boundaries and marker locations, and validate dimensions and colors. Submit only the exact serialized grid printed by that transform. On any demo mismatch, inspect the first differing cells and revise the hypothesis before rerunning the full suite. With remaining attempt budget, request a discriminating demo rather than repeatedly submitting another speculative output; if ambiguity remains at the final attempt, choose the best-supported candidate but treat it as an unverified guess internally.\n5. After an incorrect verdict, do not treat it as cell-level feedback or repeat the same grid. Revisit the ev